          "text": "{\"schema_version\":\"ai-evaluator-v2/1.1\",\"case_id\":\"2-2\",\"candidate_id\":\"candidate_2\",\"action_assessment\":{\"status\":\"preserved\",\"summary\":\"Most source activities are represented with acceptable paraphrase, including data entry/checking, contract-document retry, grid-operator interactions, customer decision, and post-switch meter/billing actions.\"},\"flow_assessment\":{\"status\":\"defect\",\"summary\":\"Most core ordering is preserved, but the graph adds unsupported precedence by making meter-data import wait for all post-switch branches, not just receipt of meter data by customer service.\"},\"control_flow_assessment\":{\"status\":\"defect\",\"summary\":\"The supplier-concurrence decision is mis-modeled: both concurrence and no-concurrence paths merge into creating the CIS contract, and the post-switch parallel section wrongly synchronizes unrelated branches before meter-data import.\"},\"main_error_type\":\"wrong_branching_or_condition\",\"explanation\":\"The decisive defect is at the grid-operator objection check. In the source, customer service creates the CIS contract only when there are no objections/no supplier concurrence. In the generated graph, the supplier-concurrence branch is merged back into the normal path and still leads to CIS contract creation, so the process can continue through contract creation even when the objection/conflict path was taken. This changes a central gating condition of the process. A secondary defect is the final join: the source allows customer service to import meter data after receiving it, while the graph forces import to wait for all concurrent billing/transmission branches, adding unsupported synchronization and precedence. Despite these issues, much of the action content and earlier sequencing is preserved.\",\"overall_score\":0.7,\"severity\":\"major\",\"confidence\":\"high\",\"ambiguity_flag\":false,\"ambiguity_explanation\":\"\",\"requires_human_review\":false,\"review_reason\":\"\"}"
        }
